  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">CONSERVATION OF SHADE COFFEE PLANTATIONS</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Shade coffee plantations provide nesting and feeding habitats for native and migratory neotropical birds. However, the canopy of many shade coffee plantations is being cut to give way to sun coffee plantations, which destroy habitat, promotes soil erosion, reduce biodiversity and increase the use of chemical fertilizers and pesticides. This project will address the conversion to sun coffee plantations in the state of Veracruz. The objectives are to: 1) train 100 smallholder coffee farmers and their families in the implementation of better practices for biodiversity conservation on their farms; 2) certify 50 farms and their products to add market value market; 3) protect 12,355 acres through a voluntary conservation program; and 5) renew and diversify the tree cover with 50,000 trees and 10 million seeds of 30 tree species that will not only increase bird habitat, but also provide farmers with alternative products such as wood, fruits, honey.</narrative>
  </description>
